# E0300: a call takes at most one unlabelled argument, and writing a second one
# does not produce "two arguments" -- it produces the OPERATOR form. Three paths
# are `receiver operator argument`, so `plain 10 3` asks for the method named
# `10` on `plain`, and a numeric literal names no method and never can.
#
# Reported at the operator, before either operand is walked: walking the lhs
# first reports `plain` as a bare function reference, which is true and useless.
plain: function {a: i64 b: i64} out i64 is {
    return a - b
}
main: function is {
    r: plain 10 3
    print "\{r}"
}
